<h2>Two statutes, two philosophies</h2>
<p>The criminal-side maintenance remedy — Section 125 CrPC, now Section 144 BNSS — is a measure against destitution, and Parliament confined it to a wife, children and parents. The Hindu Marriage Act takes a different view: matrimonial litigation should not be won or lost on the strength of the richer spouse&rsquo;s purse. Hence Section 24 arms &ldquo;either the wife or the husband&rdquo; with a claim for expenses and monthly support during proceedings, and Section 25 extends the logic to permanent alimony.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Section 144 BNSS</strong><br/>Wife, children, parents only. The husband has no claim. Summary, destitution-focused, available regardless of personal law.</div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Sections 24–25 HMA</strong><br/>Either spouse. Available only within matrimonial proceedings under the Act (divorce, judicial separation, nullity, restitution), tested against both parties&rsquo; incomes.</div>
</div>

<h2>The threshold: no independent income &ldquo;sufficient for support&rdquo;</h2>
<p>Section 24 does not ask whether the claimant earns nothing; it asks whether the claimant&rsquo;s independent income suffices for support and the necessary expenses of the proceedings. For a husband, three fact-patterns dominate the case law:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Incapacity.</strong> A husband prevented from earning by illness, accident or disability stands on the statute&rsquo;s plain terms. Medical records and treatment history carry the claim.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Genuine reversal.</strong> Business failure or job loss, coupled with demonstrated efforts to find work, can sustain a modest interim award — usually time-bound in practice, since courts expect re-employment.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Chosen idleness.</strong> An able-bodied, educated husband who declines to work is routinely refused. Courts impute earning capacity to him exactly as they impute it to husbands resisting a wife&rsquo;s claim — the street runs both ways.</div>
</div>

<h2>How courts size the award</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Bearing on a husband&rsquo;s claim</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Both parties&rsquo; affidavits of income and assets</td><td>Mandatory in matrimonial maintenance litigation; concealment cuts against the concealing party</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Earning capacity vs actual earnings</td><td>Capacity is imputed to a claimant who can work but will not</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Standard of living during marriage</td><td>Sets the frame, but does not entitle an idle spouse to parity with a working one</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Conduct of the claim</td><td>Claims filed as counter-pressure to the wife&rsquo;s cases attract close scrutiny and costs</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Permanent alimony under Section 25</h2>
<p>At the decree stage, Section 25 permits the court to order either spouse to pay the other a gross sum or periodic maintenance, having regard to the respondent&rsquo;s income and property, the claimant&rsquo;s own, the parties&rsquo; conduct and other circumstances. A husband&rsquo;s Section 25 claim is legally identical to a wife&rsquo;s; what differs is the evidentiary posture, because courts begin from the social reality that the husband is ordinarily the earning spouse and require him to displace it with proof, not assertion. Orders can be varied under Section 25(2) on change of circumstances, and rescinded under Section 25(3) on remarriage or other statutory grounds.</p>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Plead the disability, not the grievance.</strong> A husband&rsquo;s petition that catalogues the wife&rsquo;s income but is silent on why he cannot earn is dismissed on the pleadings.</p>
  <p><strong>File the income affidavit early and accurately.</strong> The claim stands or falls on the comparative financial picture.</p>
  <p><strong>Expect modesty.</strong> Even successful claims produce conservative figures; Section 24 keeps a spouse litigating, it does not fund a lifestyle.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer describes the statutory scheme in general terms for information. It is not legal advice; whether any particular claim is maintainable or prudent depends on the full facts.</div>
